artifacts.body:
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 headline: \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders.\" A purpose statement is present and the ICP (\"indie SaaS founders\") is named, but it is a category description rather than a single declarative sentence with a quantified outcome. Sequoia’s framework requires the format \"[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome].\" No quantified outcome appears in the purpose statement.",
      "notes": "Rewrite as a declarative value sentence: e.g., \"Acmecorp cuts indie SaaS founders’ tooling overhead from 20+ hrs/week to under 2 — one CLI, four integrations.\" Sequoia: define the company in a single declarative sentence with a measurable outcome."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 headline (\"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") is a strong conclusion. Slide 4 headline (\"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months)\") states facts. Slides 1, 3, 5, and 6 use topic labels or data statements rather than conclusions: \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders\"; \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI\"; \"Two founders: ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product)\"; \"Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post-money valuation.\" Mixed result across 6 slides.",
      "notes": "DocSend/Sequoia: investors skim headlines first; each should be a conclusion. Rewrite labels as conclusions: e.g., Slide 3 — \"One CLI command replaces 3 days of manual setup\" rather than the tool list."
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows Title → Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask. Problem-Solution-Proof-Ask elements are all represented. However, six slides expected in the seed framework are entirely absent: Why Now (catalyst), Market (sizing), Competition, Business Model, GTM, and Financials. The arc skeleton is present but has substantial gaps that prevent it from feeling complete or inevitable. Sequoia/DocSend/YC all cite the full Problem → Solution → Why now → Proof → Ask arc as the required standard.",
      "notes": "The core arc is there but the story stops short of establishing venture-scale upside (no market), competitive context (no competition), or a path to scale (no GTM or financials). Adding these six missing slides would complete the arc."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Traction slide is slide 4 and carries the deck’s strongest metrics: \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).\" These are the strongest proof points in the deck and they appear at exactly slide 4. DocSend data: 82% of investors who reach slide 4 finish the deck. Checked all 6 slides — no stronger proof point appears after slide 4.",
      "notes": "Well-positioned. The traction data at slide 4 follows the DocSend “Slide 4 Rule.” Good instinct to lead with real numbers early."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slides 2 (problem) and 4 (traction) are self-explanatory from headlines alone. Slide 1 (title) stands alone as positioning. Slide 3 (solution) headline — \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI\" — describes what the product is but without a workflow diagram or before/after context, the value delivered requires mental inference. Slides 5 and 6 are also bare (no names on team, no milestones on ask). DocSend: share decks must stand alone; investors spend ~2:30 of real attention.",
      "notes": "Slides 3, 5, and 6 need enough context (workflow, names, milestones) that a reader with zero background can understand the value and ask without narration."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 headline: \"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration.\" Time cost is quantified (\"20+ hours/week\"). No dollar translation, no cost of inaction, and no customer quote or discovery data point is provided. Sequoia’s framework requires quantifying the problem in time, money, or risk, and identifying the budget owner and moment of pain. Time is present; monetary impact and urgency are absent.",
      "notes": "Add a dollar translation (e.g., at a developer billing rate) and at least one customer quote or discovery anchor. The time cost is a good start but stops short of establishing urgency and a budget owner."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 headline: \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.\" Content summary: \"Solution: a single CLI that unifies GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel. No workflow diagram, screenshot, or demo described.\" Visuals: \"No visuals described.\" The description names the integrations (a feature list) but shows no before→after workflow, no product screenshot, and no 3-step flow. Sequoia/DocSend: show workflow change, not feature list; the era of Minimum Viable PowerPoint is over even at seed. Checked slide 3 visuals field: \"No visuals described.\"",
      "notes": "Rewrite as a before→after: e.g., \"Before: 3-day setup ritual for every new SaaS project. After: one CLI command, four integrations live in 10 minutes.\" Add a terminal screenshot or 3-step flow diagram."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No \"why now\" or catalyst slide exists in the 6-slide deck. The slide_reviews missing_slides list identifies \"Why now (catalyst / macro shift)\" as an important missing element. Sequoia asks: \"Why wasn’t this built before?\" — the deck provides no answer. The problem (tooling integration overhead) could have existed for years without an explanation of what changed to make this the right moment.",
      "notes": "Add a slide explaining the macro shift: the explosion of AI-assisted solo SaaS development, the proliferation of best-in-class vertical SaaS tools creating new integration overhead, or a recent platform primitive. Sequoia: ‘why now’ is a required slide, strongest at pre-seed/seed."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No market sizing slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"Market (TAM with credibility)\" as a critical missing element. The deck provides no TAM, no bottom-up sizing, no ICP count × willingness-to-pay × adoption-rate calculation. Sequoia/multiple sources: bottom-up sizing beats generic TAM charts; without market sizing, investors cannot assess whether the opportunity justifies a venture return.",
      "notes": "Add a market sizing slide with bottom-up methodology: estimate of global indie SaaS founders (ICP count) × willingness to pay × adoption rate. Include an expansion layer for adjacent segments. Even a smaller bottom-up number beats an unsubstantiated billion-dollar claim."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No competition slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"Competition / alternatives\" as a critical missing element. DocSend data: VCs spend 88% more time on the competition section in successful decks vs. unsuccessful ones. The deck makes no reference to alternatives, incumbents (Zapier, Make, Postman, GitHub Copilot), or the “do nothing” option. This is a critical omission.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping direct competitors, incumbents, and the “do nothing” option with 2–3 concrete differentiators. Never claim “no competition” — it reads as naivety to an experienced investor."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No business model slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"Business model\" as a critical missing element. The deck shows $8K MRR from 24 customers (implied ~$333/month average) but provides no pricing structure, no buyer-vs.-user distinction, no gross margin, and no model explanation. Sequoia framework: pricing model, buyer vs. user, and gross margin structure are required, especially for AI-claimed products where compute costs matter.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide covering: pricing structure (seat-based, usage-based, per-integration, or freemium-to-paid), who pays vs. who uses, gross margin estimate, and path to 60%+ gross margins. Without this, investors cannot model LTV or expansion."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No GTM slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"GTM / go-to-market\" as an important missing element. The deck has 24 paying customers but provides no explanation of how they were acquired, what channel was used, or why that channel is repeatable. Sequoia/DocSend: GTM slide must include ICP + channel + early channel proof (conversion, CAC, payback).",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide showing acquisition channels (indie hacker communities, Twitter/X, ProductHunt, GitHub), PLG motion (CLI → freemium → paid), sales cycle, ACV, and early channel proof. 24 paying customers is a good start — explain how they were found."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 content summary: \"Team: two co-founders with ex-Stripe (engineering) and ex-Notion (product) backgrounds. No names, no photos, no advisor or investor logos.\" Visuals: \"No visuals described.\" Checked slide 5 visuals field: \"No visuals described.\" Slide provides employer affiliations only; no full names, no photos, no LinkedIn links, no specific accomplishments, and no founder-market fit narrative. DocSend: team slide receives ~46 seconds (~30% of total deck attention). YC: bios and photos are required on the team slide.",
      "notes": "Add full names, photos, LinkedIn links, and one specific accomplishment per founder (e.g., what they built at Stripe or Notion at what scale). Most importantly, add a founder-market fit sentence: did these founders personally waste 20+ hours/week on tooling integration as indie SaaS builders?"
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Seed stage expected framework (from stage_profile.json): Company purpose, Problem (quantified), Solution (workflow change), Why now (catalyst), Market (TAM with credibility), Traction/proof, Competition/alternatives, Business model, GTM/go-to-market, Team, Financials (runway, burn), Ask (with milestones) — 12 elements. Deck has 6 slides covering 6 of 12: Title/Purpose, Problem, Solution, Traction, Team, Ask. Missing 6 framework elements: Why now, Market, Competition, Business model, GTM, Financials. All sources (Sequoia, DocSend, YC): pre-seed, seed, and Series A have different required structures; using only half the seed framework is a fail.",
      "notes": "The deck covers the right slides for what it has, but omits half the seed framework. Adding the 6 missing slides would bring the structure into alignment with seed-stage expectations."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 4: \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).\" Seed stage benchmarks from stage_profile.json: \"Early paying customers, $5K–$30K MRR, retention signal, 15–25% MoM growth.\" MRR ($8K), customer count (24), and growth rate (18% MoM) all fall within seed-stage expectations. However, retention/churn data is absent — required for seed-stage B2B SaaS per deck-best-practices.md seed traction requirements. NRR and CAC payback are also missing.",
      "notes": "The headline metrics are strong and stage-appropriate. The key gap is retention: even stating \"0 churn in 4 months\" would be powerful evidence if true. Add at minimum a monthly churn rate or customer retention across the 4-month window."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No financials slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"Financials (runway, burn, operating model)\" as an important missing element. Seed stage requires: operating model, revenue assumptions, gross margin, and runway (18–24 months post-raise per Carta). a16z/Carta: seed financial projection standards require at minimum revenue assumptions, gross margin, burn, and runway. The ask of $1.2M is made without any financial context.",
      "notes": "Add a financials slide with current monthly burn, post-raise runway, simple revenue model (MRR at M6/M12/M18), and gross margin estimate. A one-page operating summary is sufficient at seed — it does not need to be a full model."
    },
    {
      "id": "ask_ties_to_milestones",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 6 content summary: \"Ask: $1.2M seed round at $8M post-money valuation. No use-of-funds breakdown, no milestones tied to the raise.\" The slide states the amount and valuation but provides no use-of-funds allocation, no milestone plan (what will $1.2M accomplish?), and no runway calculation. 2026 standard per deck-best-practices.md: explicit fundraising ask + use of funds tied to milestones + next round readiness (or default-alive path) is required.",
      "notes": "Add a use-of-funds breakdown, an 18-month milestone plan (e.g., Month 6: 100 customers/$35K MRR; Month 12: $100K MRR), and a burn rate/runway calculation. This converts the ask from a number into a narrative that investors can evaluate."
    },
    {
      "id": "round_size_realistic",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Ask is \"$1.2M seed at $8M post-money valuation\" (slide 6). Checklist-criteria.md seed benchmark: $2M–$6M. Stage profile seed benchmark: \"$1M–$4M (median $2.4M, Carta H2 2024).\" At $1.2M, the raise is below the $2M–$6M standard seed range per PitchBook/NVCA + Carta 2025 benchmarks, though within the stage profile’s $1M–$4M Carta H2 2024 range. This puts $1.2M at the low end — on the edge of the typical range and not unreasonable, but the slide_review noted it is below the median and raises questions about whether it is right-sized for the milestones needed to reach Series A.",
      "notes": "Not a blocking concern but warrants milestone justification: the ask slide should show why $1.2M is sufficient to reach the next financing milestone. Without milestones, the below-median amount looks underpowered rather than capital-efficient."
    },
    {
      "id": "one_idea_per_slide",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Assessed all 6 slides. Word count estimates: slides 1–6 are 10, 12, 14, 14, 10, and 10 words respectively. Each slide headline and content summary covers a single concept: title/purpose (slide 1), problem (slide 2), solution (slide 3), traction (slide 4), team (slide 5), ask (slide 6). No slide appears to conflate two distinct ideas. Checked visuals field for slides 1–6: all show \"No visuals described\" — no complex multi-chart layouts identified.",
      "notes": "Single-idea discipline is maintained across all 6 slides. This will be gated to not_applicable by the producer script given input_format is \"text\" in deck_inventory.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "minimal_text",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Assessed all 6 slides. Word count estimates: 10, 12, 14, 14, 10, 10 words per slide — all extremely low. No slide content summary describes dense paragraphs. Checked visuals field for slides 1–6: all show \"No visuals described.\" DocSend/Storydoc: design for ~2:30 of real attention; big type, charts over text. At these word counts, no text-density issue is present in the described content.",
      "notes": "Word counts strongly suggest minimal text. This will be gated to not_applicable by the producer script given input_format is \"text\" in deck_inventory.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "slide_count_appropriate",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "deck_inventory.json total_slides: 6. Checklist-criteria.md: \"Fail: 6 or fewer, or 19 or more core slides.\" 6 slides is at the fail boundary. Storydoc data: ~10-slide decks achieve highest completion rate (32% vs. 22% average); sharp drop-off after ~18 slides. The slide_reviews missing_slides list identifies 6 additional slides that should be added (Market, Competition, Business Model, Why Now, GTM, Financials).",
      "notes": "Adding the 6 missing slides would bring the deck to 12 slides, which is in the optimal 10–12 range. This criterion will be gated to not_applicable by the producer script given input_format is \"text\" in deck_inventory.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "consistent_design",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Assessed slides 1–6. Visuals field for all slides: \"No visuals described.\" input_format is \"text\" — the deck was described in conversation rather than uploaded as a file. No visual design information (colors, fonts, spacing, layout) is available to assess consistency. Cannot confirm or deny consistent design language without rendered slides.",
      "notes": "Unable to assess consistency from text description. This criterion will be gated to not_applicable by the producer script given input_format is \"text\" in deck_inventory.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "mobile_readable",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Assessed slides 1–6. Visuals field for all slides: \"No visuals described.\" Font sizes, chart readability, and layout cannot be assessed from text description. Word counts are very low (10–14 words per slide), which is consistent with large text and minimal content, suggesting mobile readability is likely — but this cannot be confirmed without a rendered slide. Storydoc: 32% of decks opened on mobile (up to 70% for initial scans).",
      "notes": "Low word counts are a positive signal for mobile readability but visual confirmation is not possible from text input. This criterion will be gated to not_applicable by the producer script given input_format is \"text\" in deck_inventory.json."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_vague_purpose",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 headline: \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders.\" The ICP (\"indie SaaS founders\") is specific and immediately understandable. \"AI-native\" is an unsubstantiated buzzword — the deck never provides evidence of AI in the product (see ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\" in deck_inventory.json). \"Developer tools\" is a category label rather than a measurable outcome. Common-mistakes criterion: purpose must be specific, measurable, and immediately understandable; \"AI-native\" without proof is buzzwordy.",
      "notes": "The ICP specificity is a strength. Remove or substantiate \"AI-native\" and add a measurable outcome to the purpose statement. Sequoia: one declarative sentence + measurable outcome."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_nice_to_have_problem",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 headline: \"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration.\" Time cost is stated, but no cost of inaction is quantified (no dollar figure, no churn risk, no missed revenue), no budget owner is identified, and no urgency signal is given (why does this hurt now, not 3 years ago?). Common-mistakes criterion: show urgency, budget owner, and cost of inaction. The problem is real but its urgency is understated.",
      "notes": "Add a dollar translation and a cost-of-inaction sentence to establish urgency. The 20+ hours/week figure is a strong hook; it needs a financial punchline to cross from “interesting” to “urgent.”"
    },
    {
      "id": "no_hype_without_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 1 claims \"AI-native\" positioning. deck_inventory.json ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\"; ai_evidence: \"Tagline on title slide is ‘AI-native developer tools’; however no core-AI evidence is provided: no ML in value prop, no inference/training COGS, no foundation-model or fine-tuning mentions, no AI-specific retention metrics.\" Slide 3 (solution) describes the product as a CLI wrapper with no AI mechanism, model, or capability mentioned. The “AI-native” claim on slide 1 is never substantiated in any of the 6 slides. a16z AI-company framework: four core-AI signals required to substantiate AI-company positioning; none are present. Common-mistakes criterion: hype without proof reliably kills momentum.",
      "notes": "Either substantiate the \"AI-native\" claim with a specific AI capability (what model, what ML mechanism, what AI-driven outcome) or remove it from the title slide. An unverified AI claim at seed is a credibility risk, not a benefit."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_features_over_outcomes",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 content summary: \"Solution: a single CLI that unifies GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel.\" Headline: \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.\" The solution description names the integrations (feature list) and the product mechanism (\"wraps\"/\"CLI\") but does not state the outcome delivered (time saved, faster shipping, fewer breakages). Slide 2 (problem) does state an outcome (\"waste 20+ hours/week\"). Mix present: problem is outcome-focused, solution is feature-focused. Sequoia/DocSend: focus on value delivered and user outcomes, not technical architecture.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the solution slide to lead with the outcome: e.g., \"Ship your first SaaS integration in under 10 minutes.\" Follow with the mechanism (CLI, four integrations). This is a straightforward flip with high impact."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_dodged_competition",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No competition slide exists in the 6-slide deck. slide_reviews missing_slides lists \"Competition / alternatives\" as a critical missing element. The deck makes no reference to competitive alternatives, incumbents, or the \"do nothing\" option anywhere across all 6 slides. DocSend: investors scrutinize this section more than founders think; VCs spend 88% more time on competition in successful decks. Absence is equivalent to dodging.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide. Acknowledging Zapier, Make, Postman, GitHub Copilot, and manual dotfiles/scripts, and explaining concrete differentiators, is far stronger than silence. The competition section is the #1 credibility-building opportunity in the deck."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_retention_rebased",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 4 traction data: \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).\" No retention data of any kind is shown — no monthly retention rate, no churn rate, no Month 0 vs. Month 3 analysis. For a company claiming \"AI-native\" positioning, a16z requires retention analysis rebased from Month 0 to Month 3 to filter out “AI tourists.” No such analysis exists. ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\" — producer script will apply gating based on this value.",
      "notes": "If the product has AI functionality, show retention rebased from Month 3 per a16z’s AI retention benchmark framework. Even for a CLI tool, retention from M3 forward is a key investor question. Not applicable will be applied by checklist.py if ai_company_status is not ai_core."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_cost_to_serve_shown",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No COGS breakdown, compute costs, inference costs, or gross margin trajectory is shown anywhere in the 6-slide deck. The business model slide is entirely absent (missing_slides). For a company claiming \"AI-native\" positioning, a16z requires showing the path to 60%+ gross margins including what drives margin improvement (caching, model optimization, routing, pricing). ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\" — producer script will apply gating based on this value.",
      "notes": "If the product has AI/compute costs, show the COGS breakdown and margin improvement path. a16z: failure to show path to 60%+ gross margins signals an unscalable model. Not applicable will be applied by checklist.py if ai_company_status is not ai_core."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_defensibility_beyond_model",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No AI mechanism is described anywhere in the deck. Slide 3 describes the product as \"a single CLI that unifies GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\" — a wrapper architecture with no AI capability, no proprietary data, no feedback loops, no regulatory moat, and no vertical specialization described. The \"AI-native\" claim (slide 1) is entirely unsubstantiated. a16z: defensibility beyond \"we use [foundation model]\" requires at least one of: proprietary data/feedback loops, workflow distribution/embeddedness, regulatory/compliance moat, vertical specialization, or agentic capabilities. None are present. ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\" — producer script will apply gating based on this value.",
      "notes": "If AI is genuinely part of the product, describe what AI is used and what proprietary advantage it creates. a16z: an API wrapper without a moat is not defensible. Not applicable will be applied by checklist.py if ai_company_status is not ai_core."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_responsible_controls",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No mention of responsible AI practices, privacy, data handling, guardrails, safety, or compliance roadmap (SOC2, HIPAA, etc.) in any of the 6 slides. The deck is silent on all responsible AI dimensions. For an enterprise-facing AI product, a16z: responsible AI controls reduce deal friction, especially for enterprise buyers. ai_company_status: \"ai_claimed_unverified\" — producer script will apply gating based on this value.",
      "notes": "If the product handles developer credentials and API keys (GitHub, Stripe, Vercel), security and data handling are diligence questions even without AI. Add a brief note on security posture and compliance roadmap. Not applicable will be applied by checklist.py if ai_company_status is not ai_core."
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers_consistent",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Cross-checked all figures across 6 slides. Slide 4: \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months)\" — $8K MRR / 24 customers = ~$333/month average contract, a plausible price point for developer tools. Slide 6: \"$1.2M seed at $8M post-money valuation\" — consistent with an early seed raise given $8K MRR ($96K ARR run rate). No contradictions detected between slides: no differing ARR figures, no inconsistent customer counts, no mismatched growth claims. a16z: deck must be consistent with what will be shown in the data room.",
      "notes": "The numbers that exist are internally consistent. The main diligence risk is not contradiction but absence — burn rate, gross margin, and retention figures are missing entirely, leaving key metrics unverifiable."
    },
    {
      "id": "data_room_ready",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No mention of a data room, cap table, historical P&L, burn rate, or supporting materials in any of the 6 slides. The deck exists as a standalone artifact with no reference to diligence materials. a16z data room guide: cap table, historical P&L, and burn are baseline diligence items that should be referenced or implied available. Checked all 6 slide content summaries: none references supporting materials.",
      "notes": "Add a closing note or appendix reference indicating diligence materials are available: \"Data room available on request: cap table, 12-month P&L, pipeline, customer contracts.\" This signals preparedness and reduces friction."
    },
    {
      "id": "contact_info_present",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Checked all 6 slides. No email, LinkedIn, phone number, website, or other contact information appears in any slide headline, content summary, or visuals field. Slide 1 (title/cover): \"No visuals described.\" Slide 6 (ask/closing): content summary shows only the raise amount and valuation, no contact details. Pre-send checklist: contact information must be clearly visible, typically on the cover or closing slide.",
      "notes": "Add a founder email and LinkedIn URL to either the title slide (slide 1) or the ask slide (slide 6). This is the simplest fix in the deck and has zero downside."
    }
  ]
}
